 # Aspose.PDF FOSS for Python
 
-[![CI](https://github.com/aspose-pdf-foss/Aspose-PDF-FOSS-for-Python/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/aspose-pdf-foss/Aspose-PDF-FOSS-for-Python/actions/workflows/ci.yml) [![Python 3.11+](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.11%2B-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/downloads/) [![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-green.svg)](LICENSE)
+[![PyPI](https://img.shields.io/pypi/v/aspose-pdf-foss-for-python.svg)](https://pypi.org/project/aspose-pdf-foss-for-python/) ![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.11%2B-blue.svg) [![CI](https://github.com/aspose-pdf-foss/Aspose-PDF-FOSS-for-Python/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/aspose-pdf-foss/Aspose-PDF-FOSS-for-Python/actions/workflows/ci.yml) [![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-blue.svg)](LICENSE)
 
 [![Aspose.PDF FOSS for Python](https://products.aspose.org/media/pdf/python/banner-readme.png)](https://products.aspose.org/pdf/python/)
+
+Aspose.PDF FOSS for Python reads and writes PDF documents, converts them to other formats, and manipulates their content. It reads `.pdf` files and writes `.pdf`, `.svg`, `.tiff`, `.jpeg`, `.jpg`, `.png`, `.tif`, `.htm`, `.html`, `.md`, `.json`, `.csv`, `.xfdf`, and `.zip` outputs. It supports conversion to PDF/A, PDF/UA, and PDF/X standards, sanitizes documents, embeds fonts, and handles forms, annotations, bookmarks, and tables.
 
 Aspose.PDF FOSS for Python is an open-source, MIT-licensed Python library for creating, reading,
 editing, rendering, and validating PDF documents. It targets Python 3.11 and later, ships type
 information (`py.typed`), and depends only on `cryptography` and `asn1crypto` at its core. The
 project is currently in alpha, so its public APIs and feature coverage continue to evolve ahead
 of a first stable release.
-
-Page numbers in public APIs start at **1**, matching Aspose.PDF for .NET:
-`document.pages[1]` is the first page. Numeric page selections and returned
-`page_index` fields use the same numbering. Page-label collection keys retain
-.NET's zero-based convention. This is a breaking change from earlier FOSS
-versions; negative page indices and page-selection slices are no longer accepted.
-See [the split examples](examples/split_documents.py) for ten compatible workflows.
 
